Subject: gl: keep the software WebGL fallback, so a GPU-less host has a context

This is upstream of patches 0102, 0103 and 0104 in effect even though it sits
after them in the series, and the dependency is not visible in any diff: on the
product's primary deployment those three currently have nothing to act on,
because there is no WebGL context for a composed identity, a served limit table
or a served extension list to appear on.

Measured on a real headless Linux server running headed under Xvfb:
canvas.getContext("webgl") returns null. Chrome names it itself --
gpu/command_buffer/service/context_group.cc reports
ContextResult::kFatalFailure "WebGL1 blocklisted", chrome://gpu reports "WebGL
has been disabled via blocklist or the command line", and glxinfo -B under Xvfb
reports Device: llvmpipe (LLVM 20.1.2), Accelerated: no.

The mechanism, and it is not in this fork's patches. features::
IsSwiftShaderAllowed() is IsSwiftShaderAllowedByCommandLine() OR
IsSwiftShaderAllowedByFeature(). The first is true only for
--enable-unsafe-swiftshader or --use-angle=swiftshader[-for-webgl]. The second
is the kAllowSwiftShaderFallback feature, and upstream left it
FEATURE_DISABLED_BY_DEFAULT: the implicit fallback was deprecated from Chrome
130 and removed on Linux and macOS from Chrome 139. So with Mesa llvmpipe as
the only driver, the blocklist disables WebGL and nothing is allowed to take
its place. Context creation fails outright where it used to degrade silently.

Nobody caught it because --headless=new turns the fallback on for itself, so
every measurement taken headless -- which is all of them until now -- saw a
SwiftShader renderer and a working context. The opt-in is the whole difference:
bare headed gives a null context and a rejected capture, --enable-unsafe-
swiftshader gives 44 of 44 probes and an admitted capture, and
--use-gl=angle --use-angle=swiftshader gives an identical renderer.

Why this outranks a renderer string. A page that gets null from
getContext("webgl") has learned more than any string could have told it:
effectively no real desktop browser lacks WebGL, so absence identifies the
browser more surely than any value served on a context that exists. And it
lands hardest on headed-under-a-virtual-display, which is this project's
recommended maximum-stealth configuration.

The fix is the feature default, flipped to enabled, plus one console message
silenced. Three reasons it is a feature default and not
--enable-unsafe-swiftshader appended during startup:

It needs no propagation. IsSwiftShaderAllowed() is consulted in the GPU process
as well as the browser, and a compiled-in feature default is already true in
both; a switch has to be copied to every child that asks.

It leaves no argv footprint. A command line is readable by any local process,
and a browser that advertises --enable-unsafe-swiftshader in its own argv is a
tell of a different kind from the one being fixed.

And it cannot be keyed on patch 0091's GPU-less detection, which is the obvious
alternative and is wrong here. 0091's probe is one-way by design: it answers
from the presence of a DRM render node, and the reference deployment has
/dev/dri/renderD128 present with no usable driver behind it. So 0091 reports a
hardware backend on exactly the host that needs this, and a condition built on
it would miss the case. Restoring the pre-139 default covers the render-node-
present-but-unusable case that no cheap syscall can detect.

This ALLOWS the fallback; it does not force it. Selection still prefers
hardware and IsSwiftShaderAllowed() is reached only once hardware has failed,
so a host with a working GPU takes the branch it took before.
--disable-features=AllowSwiftShaderFallback turns it off.

The second hunk is required rather than cosmetic, and it is the only
page-visible part of this change. GLES2DecoderPassthroughImpl gates a console
message on the absence of the switch, so re-enabling the fallback by feature
would print "Automatic fallback to software WebGL has been deprecated..." into
the page's console on every GPU-less launch -- a page-visible observable, and
the loudest kind, since it names the deprecation in prose. The condition now
asks what the allow decision asks, features::IsSwiftShaderAllowed(), so the nag
fires when the fallback happened with no opt-in at all, which after this patch
is never. It is deliberately not deleted: reaching SwiftShader while the
fallback is disallowed would be a defect worth seeing.

Nothing else changes observably. The intended change is that WebGL exists.

Interaction with the three GPU patches, checked rather than assumed. Patch
0103's clamp keys on GetGLImplementationParts().angle !=
ANGLEImplementation::kSwiftShader, and the fallback route sets exactly that
token -- it is the same token the deprecation check above tests to recognise
the fallback, so both routes into SwiftShader, by opt-in and by fallback, are
indistinguishable to 0103's predicate. So on a GPU-less Linux host with a
context now existing, 0102 serves the hardware identity over it, 0103 serves
the claimed limits, and 0104 serves the claimed constants-only extensions.

Windows and macOS. There is no hole on Windows: IsAnySoftwareGLAllowed() ORs
IsWARPAllowed() in, kAllowD3D11WarpFallback is FEATURE_ENABLED_BY_DEFAULT, and
D3D11 WARP is a software GL path, so a GPU-less Windows host still gets a
context. macOS has the same hole as Linux, because IsWARPAllowed() returns
false off Windows and SwiftShader is the only software path there -- which
matches the removal landing on Linux and macOS first. This patch is not
platform-gated, so it covers both.

Needs a build to verify, and V3 on a GPU-less Linux host is the measurement
that closes it: the before-state is null from getContext("webgl") and the
after-state is a context carrying the composed identity. What is established
without a build is that it applies, that both translation units pass the V1
gate, that the mechanism was read out of ui/gl/gl_features.cc rather than
inferred, and that the switch route producing 44 of 44 probes was measured on
the affected host -- and the switch and the feature feed the same predicate.

Ledger: webgl.unmasked-renderer, gpu.feature-status, headless.gpu-stack.
